--- body ---
## Context

Ticket 0026 was ruled a monster on 2026-09-02 and decomposed by ladder rung;
this is rung 1 of five children, and 0026 is the tracker. Goal 1 is the
cheapest rung on the ladder (README.md, "## The goals ladder"): its assertions
need no corpus, no build and no library, so they are decidable the moment the
system is installed. The ruled method is tests first, bottom-up — a rung is
declared only when its assertions run, which is why the evidence column on
README.md is the test column.

Rescoped 2026-09-02. The author restated a standing constraint the ledger did
not carry: the acceptance harness must be applicable to any Zotero AI retrieval
tool, with zoteus the first target, Zotero core's pull request #6012 the second
and a third to be proposed. The ruling drafted for ratification the same day
(`DECISIONS.md`, awaiting) makes the harness one assertion layer phrased over a
target interface plus a thin adapter per target. This ticket therefore builds
that layer and the zoteus adapter as its first adapter, instead of extending
`bench/smoke_upstream.py` with assertions that read the zoteus server directly.

Building it does not wait on ratification, and the ticket stays unblocked: the
layer, the adapter and every assertion below run against zoteus whatever the
author rules. One thing waits, and only one. R15's uninstall clause reworded
over declared derived state is a requirement change, not a harness detail, so
this ticket asserts R15 over the adapter's declared derived-state roots and
carries that assertion's requirement text as provisional until the ruling
lands. If the author rules otherwise the assertion's requirement text changes
and its mechanism does not.

Three of goal 1's four terms are this ticket's, plus one clause that belongs to
a higher rung and is built here anyway. R10 and R15 are goal 1's; R22 stays on
goal 1's roster but is owned by ticket 0033, and R31 by ticket 0488 — neither is
this ticket's work, and the ladder's roster is not a claim about who builds it.
The fourth item is R19's normalizer sweep, which sits on goal 3 and is built
with this batch because it is the cheapest gate on the sheet (0026's log,
2026-08-31T20:08Z) and needs nothing a goal-1 assertion does not already need.
R19's other half — the two normalizers agreeing at index time — needs a built
index and stays with ticket 0580.

`bench/smoke_upstream.py` already carries two assertions against stock upstream
at the reviewed SHA in `UPSTREAM`: `check_local_by_default` (R10, the default
embedder is local, asserted against a running server rather than the source) and
`check_model_stays_in_data_dir` (R15's model-cache clause, inherited when R28
merged into R15). Both are zoteus-shaped today, and the split named in Action 2
is where they go.

## Relevant files

- `bench/smoke_upstream.py` — what this ticket splits into a layer and an
  adapter; its `check()` helper is the assertion record (id, requirement,
  clause, falsified_by).
- `bench/fold_sweep.mjs` — the R19 sweep. Today it always exits 0, prints a
  WARNING, and crashes on any tree lacking `normalizeForSearch`.
- `bench/results/smoke-1.12.0/checks.json` — where the run's verdicts land.
- `UPSTREAM` — the reviewed SHA and the repository URLs; `make upstream-checkout`
  recreates the git-ignored `fork/` at it.
- `DECISIONS.md`, the awaiting entry of 2026-09-02 — the interface, the adapter
  contract, the judge rule and the roster. Cite it; do not restate it.
- `verification/FIELD-REVIEW.md`, the Beaver entry — the source for R10's
  egress-by-design red fixture.
- `SPEC.md` §5.2.8 — the gates' specification. Cite it; do not restate it.
- `README.md`, "## Goal 1 — I can install it and take it off again" — the rung's
  roster and the level each term is decided at.

## Actions

1. The assertion layer. Every assertion is phrased over the interface's seven
   verbs — install, configure, index a declared fixture library, query in the
   three modes R33 names, stop background work, delete declared derived state,
   status — and names the verb it called plus the surface the adapter declared
   for it. No path, no file layout, no tool name. Each assertion keeps the
   record shape `check()` already carries and gains two fields: the target it
   ran against and the verb it called. A verb a target does not offer is
   declared absent by its adapter, and every assertion needing it reports "not
   offered": a third state, distinguishable from green and from red.
2. The zoteus adapter, the first one, declarations only. Per the ruling's
   contract: the derived-state roots, how the query surface is reached, how the
   target starts and stops, what "default configuration" means for zoteus, and
   which verbs it does not offer. The two existing checks split along exactly
   that line — what they assert becomes a layer assertion, what they hardcode
   becomes an adapter declaration.
3. R10's egress clause: my library text and my queries stay on this machine
   without an opt-in. Drive install, configure, index and query through the
   adapter inside a network namespace with no route out (or the platform's
   equivalent), and assert that nothing tried to leave — the run completes and
   no connection to a destination off this machine is attempted. Observing no
   egress on a machine that has a route asserts the default, not the clause.
4. R15's deletion clause over declared derived state. Event-then-state, per
   0026's log of 2026-08-31T20:08Z: call the delete verb and observe it as an
   event, then assert the state that follows — nothing the install wrote
   survives outside the adapter's declared roots. The residue sweep is what
   makes the declaration falsifiable; a declared list nobody checks for
   completeness says no more than a location does.
5. The R19 codepoint sweep, unchanged in scope and target-neutral already: it
   reads a built checkout's normalizer rather than a running target, so it needs
   no adapter and takes no interface verb. Give `fold_sweep.mjs` the
   classification fallback (`tokenize`-only when `normalizeForSearch` is absent,
   so a pre-fold tree is red by a recorded miss count and not by a crash),
   nonzero exit codes, and a writable built checkout to point at. The scripts it
   must cover are R7's, read from the sheet rather than restated here; 0026's log
   of 2026-08-31T13:38Z records why Arabic, Devanagari and Chinese break a
   Latin-plus-Cyrillic sweep's assumptions.
6. Wire the batch into the Makefile with honest states. A gate that cannot look
   reports that it could not look, and every recorded verdict names its target,
   so a green is a green for one named target rather than for the harness.

## Test

Red first per assertion, and the red state is produced rather than argued.

- **R10's egress.** Two red fixtures, the second stronger than the first. Ours
  is a configuration that opts egress in: the assertion must go red there or it
  is asserting the default. The field's is Beaver, whose paid tier syncs
  selected libraries to its own servers (`verification/FIELD-REVIEW.md`) —
  egress by design in a shipping product, so the control cannot be accidentally
  built to pass. Beaver is a control here and never a target; no adapter for it
  is in this ticket, and what this ticket owes is that the red path is exercised
  by a fixture of that shape.
- **R15's deletion.** Two reds, because the clause now reads over a
  declaration. First, an install that plants a file outside the declared roots.
  Second, an adapter that under-declares its roots: the sweep must fail it,
  otherwise the declaration grades itself.
- **R19's sweep.** A pre-fold tree: check out upstream below v1.7.2, run the
  gate, see a nonzero exit with a miss count. The fallback path is exactly the
  one no green run exercises.
- **The layer itself.** A target whose adapter declares a verb absent must
  produce "not offered" for each assertion needing it, recorded in
  `checks.json` as a state distinct from both green and red. Assert it with a
  stub adapter that declares a verb absent on purpose.

## Verification

- [ ] Every assertion names an interface verb and an adapter-declared surface;
      grepping the layer for a zoteus path, a zoteus tool name or a data-directory
      literal returns nothing.
- [ ] Each new assertion appears in `bench/results/smoke-1.12.0/checks.json`
      with its own id, requirement, clause, `falsified_by`, target and verb.
- [ ] Each was seen red once, against the fixture named in Test, before it was
      seen green.
- [ ] The absent-verb path produces a third state in `checks.json`,
      distinguishable from green and from red.
- [ ] `fold_sweep.mjs` exits nonzero on a pre-fold tree and zero against stock
      at the reviewed SHA, and neither outcome is a crash.
- [ ] `make check` green; `./tickets/erg check` green.

## Invariants

- The unit is the MUST clause, not the requirement: one assertion per clause, so
  README.md's `delivered` column becomes a count rather than a reader's
  judgement. Inherited from the tracker, ticket 0026.
- Honest red and waiver states in the Makefile, never a green that means "could
  not look". Inherited from the tracker, ticket 0026.
- The layer holds no target's name. A tool's identity lives in its adapter, and
  an assertion that mentions zoteus has left the layer.
- The adapter declares and does nothing else: no fix or workaround, no
  configuration a user would not have, no access the target does not give its
  own users, no scoring of a result.
- No judge model in this ticket. Goal 1's clauses are mechanical throughout —
  egress, residue, folding, exit codes — and the judge rule the ruling proposes
  attaches to R17, R18, R24 and golden relevance, which are other rungs' work.
- No threshold, budget or design number is restated here. SPEC.md §5.2.8 owns
  the gates and §5.2.9 the budgets; this ticket cites addresses.
- The gate runs against stock upstream at the reviewed SHA in `UPSTREAM` and
  changes no upstream code.

## Exit criteria
- [ ] the assertion layer exists, phrased over the seven interface verbs, and
      holds no target's name
- [ ] the zoteus adapter exists as declarations only, and the two assertions
      `bench/smoke_upstream.py` already carries run through it
- [ ] R10's egress clause asserted with no route out of the machine, seen red
      against an opted-in fixture
- [ ] R15's deletion clause asserted event-then-state over declared derived
      state, seen red against a planted residue and against an under-declaring
      adapter
- [ ] R19's codepoint sweep classifies instead of crashing, exits nonzero on a
      pre-fold tree, and covers the script classes R7 declares
- [ ] the batch runs from the Makefile with honest red, waiver and not-offered
      states
- [ ] README.md's goal-1 rows carry an evidence verdict that rests on something
      that ran, for the terms this ticket asserts
- [ ] not blocking, and named so its absence is not read as an omission: R15's
      requirement text reworded over declared derived state is awaiting the
      author (`DECISIONS.md`, 2026-09-02). The assertion is built now against
      the proposed phrasing; a different ruling changes its requirement text and
      not its mechanism.
